  "query_preview": "../Wednesday tp Sunday/...Active weather continues in a\ntypical Southeast Alaska fall fashion through the extended\nforecast. A weakening low anchored in the gulf dissipates as it\ndrifts north and melts into the central gulf coast by Wednesday\nmorning. General upper-level, onshore flow mid week will leave the\npanhandle experiencing showery activity, with higher potential for\nbreaks in the clouds. However, conditions are expected to\ndeteriorate Wednesday night into Thursday as another front\napproaches. Another low moving NE out of the Pacific looks to move\nover the Haida Gwaii or BC coast region Thursday; rain may cover\nthe southern panhandle, but another feature will drop into the\nnorthern gulf and track east over the northern panhandle into\nFriday. Continuous rain and gusty winds across the region could\nlast into the weekend as additional waves of moisture track across\nthe Gulf of Alaska. There looks to be a stronger low moving into\nthe western gulf with an associated frontal band arcing to the\ncentral gulf by Saturday, bringing 20 to 30 kt winds approaching\nthe gulf coastal zones Saturday to Saturday night. Expect winds to\npick up along the coast and through major marine waterways, with\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions likely starting late Wednesday\nnight. Additionally, low cloud ceilings and steady rainfall will\nreduce visibility, creating mountain obscuration and potential\ndelays for regional aviation throughout late week and the weekend.\nOverall, the end of the week's forecast will stay on the wet side\nwith fairly continuous rain, with higher amounts associated with\nthe frontal bands and lows.",
